  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sathivel vs Shriram General Insurance Co Ltd on 29 October, 2024 Author: N S Sanjay Gowda Bench: N S Sanjay Gowda -1- NC: 2024:KHC:43753 MFA No. 1442 of 2018 C/W MFA No. 1441 of 2018 IN THE HIGH COURT OF KARNATAKA AT BENGALURU DATED THIS THE 29TH DAY OF OCTOBER, 2024 BEFORE THE HON'BLE MR JUSTICE N S SANJAY GOWDA MISCELLANEOUS FIRST APPEAL NO.1442 OF 2018(MV-I) C/W MISCELLANEOUS FIRST APPEAL NO.1441 OF 2018(MV-I) IN MFA No. 1442/2018 BETWEEN: SATHIVEL S/O MUNIYAPPA, AGED ABOUT 26 YEARS, R/O NO.5/181, KURUBARAPALLI, NAGAMANGALAM, DENKANIKOTE, KRISHNAGIRI DISTRICT-635 113. ALSO AT:NO.71, III MAIN, IV CROSS, JAKKASANDRA VILLAGE, Digitally signed KORAMANGALA, BENGALURU-34 by KIRAN ...APPELLANT KUMAR R (BY SRI. GURUDEVA PRASAD K.T., ADVOCATE) Location: HIGH COURT OF KARNATAKA AND: 1. SHRIRAM GENERAL INSURANCE CO.LTD., S-5, III FLOOR, MONARCH CHAMBERS, INFANTRY ROAD, BENGALURU 560 001, BY ITS MANAGER. Sathivel vs Shriram General Insurance Co Ltd on 29 October, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65579309/ 1 2. MR. VENKATESH G., S/O GOVINDAPPA, -2- NC: 2024:KHC:43753 MFA No. 1442 of 2018 C/W MFA No. 1441 of 2018 NO.3417, HOSAHALLI MAIN ROAD, 3RD CROSS, GANDHINAGAR, MANDYA 571 401 ...RESPONDENTS (BY SRI. A.N. KRISHNA SWAMY, ADVOCATE FOR R1; SRI. S.N. BHAT, ADVOCATE FOR R2) THIS MFA IS FILED U/S 173(1) OF MV ACT AGAINST THE JUDGMENT AND AWARD DATED 04.11.2017 PASSED IN MVC NO.516/2016 ON THE FILE OF THE XIII ADDITIONAL JUDGE, COURT OF SMALL CAUSES AND MEMBER MACT, BENGALURU (SCCH15), PARTLY ALLOWING THE CLAIM PETITION FOR COMPENSATION AND SEEKING ENHANCEMENT OF COMPENSATION. IN MFA NO. 1441/2018 BETWEEN: RAMARAJ S/O MUTHU, AGED ABOUT 27 YEARS, R/AT INDLAWADI VILLAGE, KASABA HOBLI, ANEKAL TALUK 562 106. PERMANENT ADDRESS: NO.5-175, U KURUBARAPALLI, NAGAMANGALAM, VEPPANALI, KRISHNAGIRI DISTRICT-635 113 ...APPELLANT (BY SRI. GURUDEV PRASAD K.T., ADVOCATE) -3- NC: 2024:KHC:43753 MFA No. 1442 of 2018 C/W MFA No. 1441 of 2018 AND: Sathivel vs Shriram General Insurance Co Ltd on 29 October, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65579309/ 2 1. SHRIRAM GENERAL INSURANCE CO.LTD., S-5, III FLOOR, MONARCH CHAMBERS, INFANTRY ROAD, BENGALURU 560 001, BY ITS MANAGER 2. MR. VENKATESH G., S/O GOVINDAPPA, NO.3417, HOSAHALLI MAIN ROAD, 3RD CROSS, GANDHINAGAR, MANDYA 571 401. ...RESPONDENTS (BY SRI. A.N. KRISHNASWAMY, ADVOCATE FOR R1; SRI. S.N. BHAT, ADVOCATE FOR R2) THIS MFA IS FILED U/S 173(1) OF MV ACT AGAINST THE JUDGMENT AND AWARD DATED 04.11.2017 PASSED IN MVC NO.582/2016 ON THE FILE OF THE XIII ADDITIONAL JUDGE, COURT OF SMALL CAUSES AND MEMBER MACT, BENGALURU (SCCH-15), PARTLY ALLOWING THE CLAIM PETITION FOR COMPENSATION AND SEEKING ENHANCEMENT OF COMPENSATION. THESE APPEALS, COMING ON FOR ADMISSION, THIS DAY, JUDGMENT WAS DELIVERED THEREIN AS UNDER: CORAM: HON'BLE MR JUSTICE N S SANJAY GOWDA -4- NC: 2024:KHC:43753 MFA No. 1442 of 2018 C/W MFA No. 1441 of 2018 ORAL JUDGMENT 1.",
    "facts": "The claimants, being dissatisfied with the compensation awarded by the Tribunal and also exoneration of liability of the insurer, are in appeal. The Tribunal, however, recorded a finding that the driver of the tanker lorry (which was insured and which was collided with the motorcycle) was not authorized to drive a tanker lorry which was carrying hazardous goods and has, therefore, absolved the insurance company of all liability and fastened it on the owner. The Drivers' Refresher Training Course, which was issued by the Karnataka State Transport Department Officers' Association, also indicates that he had participated in a one-day Refresher Training Program as required under Section 14(2) r/w Rule 9 of CMV Rules. 12. As the claimant was aged 25 years, a multiplier of '18' will have to be applied. The Tribunal has awarded a sum of Rs.20,000/- towards 'pain and suffering' and Rs.20,000/- towards 'loss of amenities'. Since the claimant has suffered a fracture to the right foot, it would be appropriate to treat the laid-up period as three months and award a sum of Rs.27,000/- (9,000X3) towards 'loss of income during the laid-up period' as against Rs.14,000/- awarded by the Tribunal.",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the claimants points out that as per the Central Motor Vehicle Rules, 1989 (the 'CMV Rules' for short), for driving a tanker carrying hazardous goods, the only requirement is to possess a license to drive heavy goods vehicle and the driver is required to undertake a course with a specified syllabus and also undergo Refresher Training Course every year. He submits that, Sathivel vs Shriram General Insurance Co Ltd on 29 October, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65579309/ 3 NC: 2024:KHC:43753 admittedly, the driver did possess heavy goods vehicle license and the owner himself had produced the Certificate issued by the Indianoil Corporation Limited, which certified that the driver had obtained a Certificate for having undergone a Course in Hazards of Petroleum Products, Safety in Transportation of Petroleum Products by Road, Do's and Don'ts in Transportation, etc. 4. He also points out that the owner had also produced Driver's Refresher Training Course dated 10.04.2016 which indicated that he had participated in a one-day Refresher Training Program as required under Section 14(2) r/w Rule 9 of the CMV Rules and he was thus competent to drive the tanker and the exoneration of liability of the insurer was therefore incorrect. 5."
  },
